<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/><meta name="description" content="Sarah Whitaker's statistical research into the Miyazaki effect, Studio Ghibli, anime data, and quantitative similarity modeling."/><title>studio ghibli & anime similarity research · sarahwhitaker</title></head>
<body class="night-design project-page report-page"><AnimatedBackground/><main>
<header class="project-hero"><a class="back-link" href="/">♡ back home</a><p class="project-kicker">STATISTICAL RESEARCH</p><h1>Studio Ghibli &amp; Anime Similarity Research</h1><div class="floral-divider" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><b>✿</b><span></span></div><p class="project-subtitle">bayesian analysis · data integration · similarity modeling · R</p></header>
<article class="report-content">
<section class="report-intro"><p class="section-label">THE PROJECT</p><h2>From the Miyazaki effect to a way of measuring similarity across anime.</h2><p>This project started as a class assignment about Studio Ghibli, but I kept working on it because I wanted to explore a bigger question: if Miyazaki and Studio Ghibli had a meaningful influence on anime, could that influence be observed through measurable similarities between films?</p></section>
<section class="report-section"><p class="section-label">01 · ORIGINAL STUDY</p><div class="stats"><div><b>23</b><span>Ghibli films</span></div><div><b>11</b><span>Miyazaki-directed</span></div><div><b>$81M</b><span>mean revenue</span></div><div><b>$41M</b><span>median revenue</span></div></div><p>The original analysis asked whether Hayao Miyazaki-directed Studio Ghibli films had higher revenue than films directed by others. The dataset included film name, year, director, screenplay writer, budget, revenue, genres, and duration.</p></section>
<section class="report-section"><p class="section-label">02 · BAYESIAN ANALYSIS</p><div class="method-grid"><div><b>01 · PRIOR</b><h3>Gamma-Gamma</h3><p>Used α = 2 and β = 0.02, corresponding to a prior mean of $100M.</p></div><div><b>02 · POSTERIOR</b><h3>Revenue estimates</h3><p>Updated the model using the observed revenue of each director group.</p></div><div><b>03 · SIMULATION</b><h3>Monte Carlo</h3><p>Used simulation to examine the posterior distributions and uncertainty.</p></div></div><div class="chart"><h3>Posterior expected revenue</h3><div class="row"><span>Miyazaki</span><i><em style="width:100%"></em></i><b>$104.7M</b></div><div class="row"><span>Other directors</span><i><em style="width:46.5%"></em></i><b>$48.7M</b></div></div></section>
<section class="report-section"><p class="section-label">03 · THE MIYAZAKI EFFECT</p><h2 class="subhead">The question grew from Miyazaki's impact within Ghibli to his possible influence on anime more broadly.</h2><p>The revenue study gave me a way to think about Miyazaki's impact within Studio Ghibli. I then wanted to ask a broader research question: if Miyazaki had a meaningful influence on anime beyond Ghibli, could films that share more characteristics with Ghibli provide quantitative evidence consistent with that influence?</p><div class="finding">✦ Similarity is not the same thing as causation. The goal was to build a measurable way to explore patterns that could be consistent with influence, not to claim that similarity alone proves where an anime's ideas came from.</div></section>
<section class="report-section"><p class="section-label">04 · BUILDING THE ANIME DATASET</p><div class="pipeline"><span>multiple data sources</span><b>→</b><span>title matching &amp; cleaning</span><b>→</b><span>merged dataset</span><b>→</b><span>analysis-ready features</span></div><p>I continued the project outside of the original class assignment, combining several anime and movie data sources and working through the messy part of making them usable together: standardizing titles, correcting mismatches, cleaning fields, joining datasets, and restructuring genre and theme information.</p><p>The resulting dataset combined movie information with anime-specific variables such as scores, genres, themes, demographics, setting, and character attributes. I also explored differences between Ghibli and non-Ghibli films through exploratory visualizations.</p></section>
<section class="report-section"><p class="section-label">05 · SIMILARITY MODELING</p><div class="method-grid"><div><b>01 · FEATURES</b><h3>Mixed data</h3><p>Numeric and categorical characteristics were prepared together for comparison.</p></div><div><b>02 · GOWER DISTANCE</b><h3>Measure distance</h3><p>Gower distance was used because the dataset contains mixed variable types.</p></div><div><b>03 · SIMILARITY</b><h3>1 − distance</h3><p>Distances were converted into a similarity matrix, with higher values representing more similar films.</p></div></div><p>The resulting matrix gave each pair of anime a similarity score based on the characteristics included in the dataset. I used it as a way to explore which films looked most similar to one another across multiple dimensions.</p></section>
<section class="report-section"><p class="section-label">06 · EXPLORING THE RESULTS</p><p>I visualized the similarity matrix with heatmaps and created a separate view of the top 20 most similar anime pairs. To make the pair analysis more useful, I removed self-similarity and filtered out pairs that shared the same first title word before selecting the highest-scoring matches.</p><div class="finding">✦ The goal was not simply to find a single “most Ghibli” anime. It was to build a quantitative framework for comparing films and use those patterns to explore the broader question behind the project.</div></section>
<section class="report-section"><p class="section-label">07 · WHAT I LEARNED</p><div class="finding">✦ Data analysis often becomes a data-engineering problem first. Much of the work was cleaning, matching, reshaping, and validating information from different sources.</div><div class="finding">✦ Gower distance provided a way to compare observations containing both numeric and categorical features.</div><div class="finding">✦ A project can evolve when the first question leads to a more interesting one. What started as a focused revenue analysis became an exploration of how to quantify similarity across anime.</div></section>
<section class="report-section"><p class="section-label">08 · TAKEAWAY</p><p>This project shows the progression from a focused Bayesian analysis to a broader research workflow: start with a question about Miyazaki's impact, build and clean the data, model measurable similarities, visualize the results, and use those results to explore a larger question about influence.</p></section>
</article>
